<header class="sp-head"><a class="sp-portrait" href="/encyclopedia/winston-churchill" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/winston-churchill.jpg" alt="" width="104" height="104"></a><p class="featured-region">5 March 1946 · Fulton, Missouri</p><h1 class="sp-title">Churchill: An Iron Curtain Has Descended</h1><p class="sp-by"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winston-churchill">Winston Churchill</a></p></header>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>The setting</h2><p>Voted out of office in July 1945, Winston Churchill accepted an invitation to speak at Westminster College in Fulton, Missouri, and President Harry Truman sat on the platform beside him. Under the title 'The Sinews of Peace', Churchill warned that Soviet control was hardening over Eastern Europe and called for a special relationship between Britain and the United States.</p></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>Key passage</h2><blockquote class="sp-quote"><p>From Stettin in the Baltic to Trieste in the Adriatic, an iron curtain has descended across the Continent.</p></blockquote><p class="sp-source">'The Sinews of Peace', Westminster College, Fulton, Missouri, 5 March 1946. Quoted briefly: the full text is still under copyright.</p><div class="sp-full"><a class="sp-full-link" href="https://winstonchurchill.org/resources/speeches/1946-1963-elder-statesman/the-sinews-of-peace/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the complete text<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a><span class="sp-full-src">International Churchill Society</span><button type="button" class="sp-share" data-share-text="“From Stettin in the Baltic to Trieste in the Adriatic, an iron curtain has descended across the Continent.” — Winston Churchill, Churchill: An Iron Curtain Has Descended" data-share-img="/images/og/speech-churchill-iron-curtain.jpg">Share this quote</button></div></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>What followed</h2><p>Stalin denounced the speech as a call to war, and many in the West thought it alarmist. Within little more than a year the Truman Doctrine and the Marshall Plan had committed the United States to containing Soviet power in Europe, NATO followed in 1949, and the 'iron curtain' gave its name to the dividing line of the Cold War for more than forty years.</p><div class="sp-led"><h3 class="sp-led-k">What it led to</h3><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-curtain">Iron Curtain</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/truman-doctrine">Truman Doctrine</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marshall-plan">Marshall Plan</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a></div></div></section>
